863
00:31:55,120 --> 00:31:58,559
Continuing from our previous

864
00:31:56,480 --> 00:32:00,559
discussions, let's dive into the physics

865
00:31:58,559 --> 00:32:03,039
of X-ray imaging and how we can collect

866
00:32:00,559 --> 00:32:05,600
and process data to reconstruct an

867
00:32:03,039 --> 00:32:07,679
image. In X-ray imaging, we start with

868
00:32:05,600 --> 00:32:10,240
the concept of a line integral.

869
00:32:07,679 --> 00:32:12,159
Essentially, the X-rays pass through the

870
00:32:10,240 --> 00:32:15,039
object and what we measure is the energy

871
00:32:12,159 --> 00:32:16,960
of penetrated X-ray photons and then

872
00:32:15,039 --> 00:32:19,679
compute the line integral along the

873
00:32:16,960 --> 00:32:22,480
X-ray path. Such line integrals together

874
00:32:19,679 --> 00:32:24,240
form the radon transform. To make it

875
00:32:22,480 --> 00:32:25,919
simpler, imagine you have an object and

876
00:32:24,240 --> 00:32:27,840
you put all line integrals from a

877
00:32:25,919 --> 00:32:29,919
specific direction to form an X-ray

878
00:32:27,840 --> 00:32:31,679
projection. This projection is a

879
00:32:29,919 --> 00:32:33,279
one-dimensional profile which is the

880
00:32:31,679 --> 00:32:35,360
information you can get along all the

881
00:32:33,279 --> 00:32:38,080
X-ray paths passing through the object

882
00:32:35,360 --> 00:32:40,000
in that direction.

883
00:32:38,080 --> 00:32:42,880
Now let's take this idea into the realm

884
00:32:40,000 --> 00:32:46,320
of mathematics. We start with a 2D image

885
00:32:42,880 --> 00:32:48,240
denoted as fxy.

886
00:32:46,320 --> 00:32:50,799
When we have all X-ray projections or

887
00:32:48,240 --> 00:32:53,200
the radon transform, we get a new 2D

888
00:32:50,799 --> 00:32:55,679
function P theta T where T is the

889
00:32:53,200 --> 00:32:59,440
coordinate along the 1D detector array

890
00:32:55,679 --> 00:33:01,600
and theta is the projection orientation.

891
00:32:59,440 --> 00:33:03,840
By changing the angle theta, we rotate

892
00:33:01,600 --> 00:33:06,320
the data acquisition system to gather

893
00:33:03,840 --> 00:33:08,320
X-ray projections.

894
00:33:06,320 --> 00:33:10,480
Now the magic happens when we flip the

895
00:33:08,320 --> 00:33:12,159
process around. Given this collected

896
00:33:10,480 --> 00:33:15,440
projection data set, our goal is to

897
00:33:12,159 --> 00:33:17,200
reconstruct the original 2D image f(xy).

898
00:33:15,440 --> 00:33:19,039
This is the inverse process. And here's

899
00:33:17,200 --> 00:33:21,600
where things get interesting. By perform

900
00:33:19,039 --> 00:33:23,519
the 1D forier trans of each and every of

901
00:33:21,600 --> 00:33:25,760
the 1D projections, we move into the

902
00:33:23,519 --> 00:33:28,000
forier domain.

903
00:33:25,760 --> 00:33:29,840
To put it simply, the 1D projections we

904
00:33:28,000 --> 00:33:32,720
collect represent profiles in the

905
00:33:29,840 --> 00:33:34,240
frequency domain. By rotating the data

906
00:33:32,720 --> 00:33:36,559
acquisition system, we eventually

907
00:33:34,240 --> 00:33:38,559
capture the entire forier space. And

908
00:33:36,559 --> 00:33:40,720
once we have all the data in the forier

909
00:33:38,559 --> 00:33:43,039
space, we can apply the inverse forier

910
00:33:40,720 --> 00:33:46,000
transform to reconstruct the original

911
00:33:43,039 --> 00:33:48,399
image. This process connects the data

912
00:33:46,000 --> 00:33:50,640
from the physical object to the forier

913
00:33:48,399 --> 00:33:52,720
spectrum of the underlying image and

914
00:33:50,640 --> 00:33:55,600
from the forier spectrum we can

915
00:33:52,720 --> 00:33:57,440
reconstruct the original image. While

916
00:33:55,600 --> 00:33:59,279
this overview gives you the fundamental

917
00:33:57,440 --> 00:34:01,519
idea, later in the course we'll dive

918
00:33:59,279 --> 00:34:03,360
deeper into the forier transform, why

919
00:34:01,519 --> 00:34:05,600
they can be inverted and how this

920
00:34:03,360 --> 00:34:08,399
mathematical framework ties directly to

921
00:34:05,600 --> 00:34:10,720
the imaging process. But for now, keep

922
00:34:08,399 --> 00:34:13,119
in mind that the forward process, how we

923
00:34:10,720 --> 00:34:15,119
collect data, and the inverse process,

924
00:34:13,119 --> 00:34:17,119
how we reconstruct the image, are two

925
00:34:15,119 --> 00:34:20,639
sides of the same coin, closely linked

926
00:34:17,119 --> 00:34:22,639
by fur analysis.

927
00:34:20,639 --> 00:34:24,720
Continuing with our exploration of

928
00:34:22,639 --> 00:34:27,839
imaging modalities, let's now discuss

929
00:34:24,720 --> 00:34:29,839
posetron emission tomography or PET.

930
00:34:27,839 --> 00:34:32,000
While the previous imaging techniques

931
00:34:29,839 --> 00:34:34,800
focused on capturing the anatomical

932
00:34:32,000 --> 00:34:36,960
structure, PET offers a powerful way to

933
00:34:34,800 --> 00:34:40,320
visualize the functional activity in the

934
00:34:36,960 --> 00:34:42,079
human body. So, how does it work? Well,

935
00:34:40,320 --> 00:34:44,800
the key to PET is the introduction of

936
00:34:42,079 --> 00:34:46,480
radioactive tracers into the body. These

937
00:34:44,800 --> 00:34:48,000
tracers are typically radioactive

938
00:34:46,480 --> 00:34:50,399
chemicals that participate in your

939
00:34:48,000 --> 00:34:52,320
body's metabolic processes.

940
00:34:50,399 --> 00:34:55,520
For example, tumors tend to consume a

941
00:34:52,320 --> 00:34:58,320
lot of glucose. If we couple glucose

942
00:34:55,520 --> 00:35:00,560
molecules with a radioactive tracer, we

943
00:34:58,320 --> 00:35:02,480
can track how much glucose the tumor is

944
00:35:00,560 --> 00:35:04,960
using, which directly correlates with

945
00:35:02,480 --> 00:35:06,960
its activity. Tumors, which are often

946
00:35:04,960 --> 00:35:09,280
highly metabolic, will naturally absorb

947
00:35:06,960 --> 00:35:11,440
more of this tracer.

948
00:35:09,280 --> 00:35:13,520
Once the PET tracer is in the body, it

949
00:35:11,440 --> 00:35:16,079
begins to emit gammaray photons in

950
00:35:13,520 --> 00:35:18,480
pairs. And two photons in a pair will

951
00:35:16,079 --> 00:35:20,640
travel in opposite directions. Gamma

952
00:35:18,480 --> 00:35:22,160
rays, much like X-rays, can penetrate

953
00:35:20,640 --> 00:35:23,680
through tissues and be detected

954
00:35:22,160 --> 00:35:25,839
externally.

955
00:35:23,680 --> 00:35:27,839
When we receive gamma ray photons from

956
00:35:25,839 --> 00:35:29,920
two different detector locations, we

957
00:35:27,839 --> 00:35:31,920
know they originated from the same event

958
00:35:29,920 --> 00:35:33,920
somewhere on the line connecting the two

959
00:35:31,920 --> 00:35:35,520
detectors.

960
00:35:33,920 --> 00:35:37,599
This process is similar to the

961
00:35:35,520 --> 00:35:40,800
measurement of line integrals used for

962
00:35:37,599 --> 00:35:43,520
CT. Then we can apply Furier analysis to

963
00:35:40,800 --> 00:35:45,280
reconstruct the images in PET. Furier

964
00:35:43,520 --> 00:35:46,880
transforms help us create detailed

965
00:35:45,280 --> 00:35:49,359
functional images of the body's

966
00:35:46,880 --> 00:35:51,040
processes. The goal here is to

967
00:35:49,359 --> 00:35:53,520
understand how much activities are

968
00:35:51,040 --> 00:35:55,520
happening at specific locations. So

969
00:35:53,520 --> 00:35:58,240
while we've seen how CT gives us

970
00:35:55,520 --> 00:36:00,560
anatomical details, PET allows us to

971
00:35:58,240 --> 00:36:03,760
observe the biological functions like

972
00:36:00,560 --> 00:36:06,079
metabolism within those structures.

973
00:36:03,760 --> 00:36:08,560
This modality is especially useful for

974
00:36:06,079 --> 00:36:10,480
identifying cancerous tissues, studying

975
00:36:08,560 --> 00:36:12,880
brain function, and even monitoring

976
00:36:10,480 --> 00:36:15,119
heart conditions. Later, we'll explain

977
00:36:12,880 --> 00:36:17,200
how the gammaray photons are detected

978
00:36:15,119 --> 00:36:20,079
and processed to reconstruct these

979
00:36:17,200 --> 00:36:22,480
functional images. But for now, remember

980
00:36:20,079 --> 00:36:24,480
that PET imaging provides us with a way

981
00:36:22,480 --> 00:36:26,480
to look inside the body, not just for

982
00:36:24,480 --> 00:36:30,560
its structure, but also for how

983
00:36:26,480 --> 00:36:32,400
physiology is actively working.

984
00:36:30,560 --> 00:36:34,240
Now, let's explore a closely related

985
00:36:32,400 --> 00:36:36,480
imaging technique in nuclear medicine

986
00:36:34,240 --> 00:36:39,839
called single photon emission computed

987
00:36:36,480 --> 00:36:42,480
tomography or spec. While posatron

988
00:36:39,839 --> 00:36:44,560
emission tomography PET relies on the

989
00:36:42,480 --> 00:36:46,880
detection of paired gammaray photons

990
00:36:44,560 --> 00:36:49,599
emitted from the tracer, spec works

991
00:36:46,880 --> 00:36:51,680
differently. In spec, the radio tracer

992
00:36:49,599 --> 00:36:53,839
emits gammaray photons randomly and

993
00:36:51,680 --> 00:36:55,680
individually without being paired.

994
00:36:53,839 --> 00:36:57,599
Essentially, instead of detecting two

995
00:36:55,680 --> 00:36:59,920
photons coming from opposite directions,

996
00:36:57,599 --> 00:37:01,920
as we do in PET, we measure single

997
00:36:59,920 --> 00:37:04,880
gammaray photons emitted from a

998
00:37:01,920 --> 00:37:06,560
radioactive tracer within the body. When

999
00:37:04,880 --> 00:37:08,480
a photon is detected through a

1000
00:37:06,560 --> 00:37:10,240
columator, we know it came from a

1001
00:37:08,480 --> 00:37:12,320
particular path through the patient

1002
00:37:10,240 --> 00:37:14,240
body. But since the photons are not

1003
00:37:12,320 --> 00:37:16,400
paired, we need to use different data

1004
00:37:14,240 --> 00:37:19,200
pre-processing and image reconstruction

1005
00:37:16,400 --> 00:37:20,640
methods. Spec provides valuable

1006
00:37:19,200 --> 00:37:23,520
information, but it's a bit different

1007
00:37:20,640 --> 00:37:25,359
from PET. In spec, we capture these

1008
00:37:23,520 --> 00:37:27,359
single photons from various angles

1009
00:37:25,359 --> 00:37:29,599
around the body and use dedicated

1010
00:37:27,359 --> 00:37:32,160
tomographic reconstruction techniques to

1011
00:37:29,599 --> 00:37:34,240
create an image. We'll discuss the

1012
00:37:32,160 --> 00:37:36,160
details of spec imaging later in the

1013
00:37:34,240 --> 00:37:38,560
course, but for now, keep in mind that

1014
00:37:36,160 --> 00:37:40,240
while PET relies on paired photons to

1015
00:37:38,560 --> 00:37:43,119
provide highly precise spatial

1016
00:37:40,240 --> 00:37:45,040
information, SPEC uses single photons to

1017
00:37:43,119 --> 00:37:47,839
gather functional information in a

1018
00:37:45,040 --> 00:37:49,920
different way. Both PET and SPEC produce

1019
00:37:47,839 --> 00:37:52,640
tomographic images of radioracer

1020
00:37:49,920 --> 00:37:54,240
activities.

1021
00:37:52,640 --> 00:37:56,640
Now, let's turn our attention to the

1022
00:37:54,240 --> 00:37:59,440
third major imaging modality, magnetic

1023
00:37:56,640 --> 00:38:01,680
resonance imaging or MRI.

1024
00:37:59,440 --> 00:38:03,839
MRI uses a completely different approach

1025
00:38:01,680 --> 00:38:06,480
compared to X-ray and nuclear imaging

1026
00:38:03,839 --> 00:38:09,040
techniques, including PET and SPECT. In

1027
00:38:06,480 --> 00:38:11,839
MRI, we utilize radio frequency signals

1028
00:38:09,040 --> 00:38:13,920
to gather detailed images of the body.

1029
00:38:11,839 --> 00:38:15,920
Here's how it works. First, we subject

1030
00:38:13,920 --> 00:38:18,320
the patient to a very strong magnetic

1031
00:38:15,920 --> 00:38:20,640
field. This magnetic field aligns the

1032
00:38:18,320 --> 00:38:23,280
hydrogen nuclei, which are abundant in

1033
00:38:20,640 --> 00:38:24,960
water molecules in your body. You can

1034
00:38:23,280 --> 00:38:27,839
think of it like tiny compass needles

1035
00:38:24,960 --> 00:38:29,359
scattered randomly. Without a magnetic

1036
00:38:27,839 --> 00:38:32,160
field, they all point in different

1037
00:38:29,359 --> 00:38:34,240
directions. But once the strong magnetic

1038
00:38:32,160 --> 00:38:36,400
field is applied, most of them line up

1039
00:38:34,240 --> 00:38:38,079
neatly, just like compasses pointing

1040
00:38:36,400 --> 00:38:40,480
north.

1041
00:38:38,079 --> 00:38:42,240
Then we introduce a radio frequency RF

1042
00:38:40,480 --> 00:38:44,480
signal that briefly disrupts this

1043
00:38:42,240 --> 00:38:46,240
alignment, causing the hydrogen nuclei

1044
00:38:44,480 --> 00:38:48,320
to emit their own radio frequency

1045
00:38:46,240 --> 00:38:51,359
signals as they return to their original

1046
00:38:48,320 --> 00:38:53,280
stable state. The key advantage of MRI

1047
00:38:51,359 --> 00:38:54,800
is that it is sensitive to the water

1048
00:38:53,280 --> 00:38:56,960
content in different tissues and

1049
00:38:54,800 --> 00:38:59,280
interactions of hydrogen nuclei with

1050
00:38:56,960 --> 00:39:01,040
their micro environments which makes MRI

1051
00:38:59,280 --> 00:39:02,800
extremely useful for imaging soft

1052
00:39:01,040 --> 00:39:05,680
tissues like the brain, muscles and

1053
00:39:02,800 --> 00:39:07,920
organs. The signal we collect can give

1054
00:39:05,680 --> 00:39:10,320
us a lot of valuable information such as

1055
00:39:07,920 --> 00:39:12,079
chemical shifts in tissues which help us

1056
00:39:10,320 --> 00:39:14,320
distinguish between different types of

1057
00:39:12,079 --> 00:39:16,800
tissues and even detect abnormalities

1058
00:39:14,320 --> 00:39:18,880
like tumors or inflammation.

1059
00:39:16,800 --> 00:39:20,880
MRI is also great for studying brain

1060
00:39:18,880 --> 00:39:23,520
activity as the technique can be used to

1061
00:39:20,880 --> 00:39:25,440
observe changes in blood flow over time.

1062
00:39:23,520 --> 00:39:27,200
This makes MRI not only a structural

1063
00:39:25,440 --> 00:39:28,480
imaging tool but also a functional one

1064
00:39:27,200 --> 00:39:30,960
adding a layer of depth to our

1065
00:39:28,480 --> 00:39:33,680
understanding of the body. Together with

1066
00:39:30,960 --> 00:39:36,160
CT and nuclear imaging techniques, MRI

1067
00:39:33,680 --> 00:39:37,680
forms the trio of most important imaging

1068
00:39:36,160 --> 00:39:40,240
modalities.

1069
00:39:37,680 --> 00:39:42,640
While CT focuses on capturing detailed

1070
00:39:40,240 --> 00:39:44,800
anatomical structures using X-rays and

1071
00:39:42,640 --> 00:39:47,520
nuclear imaging provides insight into

1072
00:39:44,800 --> 00:39:50,079
physiological functions, MRI offers

1073
00:39:47,520 --> 00:39:52,400
unmatched soft tissue contrast and the

1074
00:39:50,079 --> 00:39:55,040
ability to examine both structure and

1075
00:39:52,400 --> 00:39:58,960
function in a non-invasive way, even

1076
00:39:55,040 --> 00:40:01,119
into our thinking processes.

1077
00:39:58,960 --> 00:40:02,560
Now, let's look at ultrasound imaging.

1078
00:40:01,119 --> 00:40:04,320
While it might not be as widely

1079
00:40:02,560 --> 00:40:07,359
discussed or as advanced as the big

1080
00:40:04,320 --> 00:40:08,960
three, CT, nuclear imaging, and MRI,

1081
00:40:07,359 --> 00:40:11,119
ultrasound remains a powerful,

1082
00:40:08,960 --> 00:40:13,200
affordable, and safe imaging tool in

1083
00:40:11,119 --> 00:40:15,359
medical practice and seems playing an

1084
00:40:13,200 --> 00:40:18,000
increasingly important role for point of

1085
00:40:15,359 --> 00:40:19,599
care imaging. So, how does ultrasound

1086
00:40:18,000 --> 00:40:21,359
work?

1087
00:40:19,599 --> 00:40:23,839
It operates based on the principle of

1088
00:40:21,359 --> 00:40:25,520
mechanical vibrations. In ultrasound

1089
00:40:23,839 --> 00:40:27,760
imaging, we use a device called a

1090
00:40:25,520 --> 00:40:30,880
transducer, which contains PZO electric

1091
00:40:27,760 --> 00:40:33,040
materials. These materials can generate

1092
00:40:30,880 --> 00:40:35,200
highfrequency sound waves when subjected

1093
00:40:33,040 --> 00:40:37,839
to an electric current. These sound

1094
00:40:35,200 --> 00:40:39,599
waves are then sent into the body. As

1095
00:40:37,839 --> 00:40:41,280
the sound waves travel through tissues,

1096
00:40:39,599 --> 00:40:43,599
they interact with various structures

1097
00:40:41,280 --> 00:40:45,280
inside the body. Some waves are

1098
00:40:43,599 --> 00:40:48,000
reflected and others continue through

1099
00:40:45,280 --> 00:40:50,320
the tissues. The reflected waves are

1100
00:40:48,000 --> 00:40:52,640
detected by the transducer. And from

1101
00:40:50,320 --> 00:40:55,040
this data, we can reconstruct an image

1102
00:40:52,640 --> 00:40:56,880
of the internal structures. Essentially,

